import Scry.Egraph
/-!
# Scry.EgraphNative: compatibility import for the one e-graph engine

This module used to be the native-table backend of the S12 e-graph, a second
implementation that declared the same names as `lib/Scry/Egraph.wiz` (74 of the
reference module's 80 declarations, plus the analyses, explanations, limits and `Option`
costs the reference lacked). The two could not be imported into one program.

They are now one engine. `lib/Scry/Egraph.wiz` keeps the reference surface (`EGraph`,
`ENode`, `ETerm`, `Pat`, `Rewrite`, `costs : U64Array` with `EGraph.inf`, and
`applyAt`/`applyMatches` without a rule index) on the native representation: `etable` is
a `U64Map` from an e-node hash to a bucket, so `add` and `rebuild` do one bucket lookup
per node instead of scanning a flat association list. The analyses (`AnSpec`,
`EClassAn`, `saturateAn`), explanations (`UnionStep`, `explain`), limits
(`saturateLimited`) and the `Option` cost view (`costsOpt`/`costOpt`) are part of that
module too.

`import Scry.EgraphNative` therefore just imports `Scry.Egraph` (Wiz imports are
transitive, and top-level names are global, so every `Scry.*` name is available through
either import). It exists so the modules and examples that named this import —
`Scry.EqPlan` and `examples/scry-egraph-{analysis,explain,extract-opt,limits}.wiz` keep
working and so a reader looking for the old backend lands on this note. There is no
second implementation, no separate record type, and no import split: both names can be
imported into one program, and a whole-library loader imports every module of `lib/` at
once.

## What still differs from the old native module

* The record has six fields (`parent`, `rank`, `nodes : List (Nat × ENode)`,
  `etable : U64Map (List (ENode × Nat))`, `next`, `log`), not the native seven: `nodes`
  is the reference's list (newest first) and there is no separate `nseq`. The list is
  what `Scry.EgraphWil` reads, so the Wil layer needed no change.
* `costs`/`cost` are the reference `Nat`/`inf` forms, not `Option Nat`. The native
  `Option` forms are `costsOpt`/`costOpt`; `examples/scry-egraph-extract-opt.wiz` uses
  them, and its digest is unchanged.
* `applyAt`/`applyMatches` are the reference forms (rule `0` in the union log). The
  rule-index forms explanations need are `applyAtN`/`applyMatchesN`; `applyRules`
  records each rule's index. `applyMatchesGuarded` is unchanged (it already took the
  rule index).
* `mergeRows` still grows the union-find for ids a fact names before interning them, as
  the native module did; with interned ids it is the reference behaviour.

The six differential `examples/scry-egraph-*.wiz` cases, the reconstruction and
extraction cases, and the analysis, explanation and limit cases all produce their
previous digests; the operations they use are the same, only the table and cost
representations moved.
-/
